\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{A negative second-neighbor coefficient}
\date{2026-11-06}

\p{Take #{R=2}, #{c_1=\frac{4}{3}} and #{c_2=-\frac{1}{12}},
in the notation of the
[finite-range circle precision](fdip-00C8). The second-order
coefficient is
##{\kappa=\frac{4}{3}+4\cdot\Bigl(-\frac{1}{12}\Bigr)=1.}
The symbol is
##{\alpha(\theta)=\frac{8}{3}(1-\cos\theta)
-\frac{1}{6}(1-\cos 2\theta).}
The identities #{1-\cos\theta=2\sin^2(\frac{\theta}{2})} and
#{1-\cos 2\theta=2\sin^2\theta} give
##{\alpha(\theta)=\frac{4}{3}\sin^2\frac{\theta}{2}
\Bigl(3+\sin^2\frac{\theta}{2}\Bigr).}
The right-hand side is positive when #{\theta} is not an
integer multiple of #{2\pi}. Thus a negative coefficient is
compatible with the standing assumptions, and here
#{\kappa=1}.}
